 "description": "<p style=\"font-size: 18px; line-height: 21.6px; background-color: rgb(233, 237, 235);\"><strong>Absolute gravity stations in Greenland</strong></p><p style=\"font-size: 18px; line-height: 21.6px; background-color: rgb(233, 237, 235);\">Point near GNET GNSS antenna next to the landing strip. Contact the Geodynamics department at DTU Space for more information.</p><p style=\"font-size: 18px; line-height: 21.6px; background-color: rgb(233, 237, 235);\">The absolute gravity stations are basis stations in the national gravity network of Greenland. These basis stations are the backbone in the densification of gravity measurements in an area used for geoid determination or exploration for natural resources.</p><p style=\"font-size: 18px; line-height: 21.6px; background-color: rgb(233, 237, 235);\">The stations are also used for scientific research by repeated measuring at the stations.&nbsp;These repeated measurements can be used for studies of geodynamics in Greenland, e.g. the study of glacial isostatic adjustment in connection with the ongoing climatic changes.</p>",
